    },
    {
      "id": "0cf8X_-7O66Sjk8asJpyn",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -3729.416905763873,
      "y": 2198.972676888549,
      "width": 1925.518798828125,
      "height": 425,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a7",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 518634439,
      "version": 1880,
      "versionNonce": 153947913,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783524621612,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. Handle users who are following large number of users?\n- If a user follows large nos of users, queries to Follow table takes time\n- Now for each of those users, we make large nos of queries to Posts table to build the feed\n- This is called, fan-out on read i.e. a single read req fans out to create many more requests\n- The solution is to pre-compute the feed at the time of write or post creation, rather than at the time of read.\n- This is called fan-out on write - instead of assembling feed when user asks for it, we precompute feed when posts are created.\n- Natural question is what if a user has large nos of followers, well a Product decision is required to limit no of followers, FB does it with limit of 5000 friends\n- As a user with 100k follows doesn't notice if their posts are appearing couple of mins late.\n- Have a PrecomputedFeed table(userId(PK), createdAt(SK), postId[]), if new post created just add to relevant feeds.\n- postId is a list in reverse chronological order, limited to small nos of posts say 200 or so, keeping space minimum.\n- What if user user pageback to 100s of pages and we end on our pre-computed feed? There's always a cap, even google does that, doesn't allows you to reach the end as real users don't do that\n- Even so, we can always query Follow and Posts table as fallback.\n- postId(10 bytes) * 200(posts stored per user) * 2B(total no of user) = 4TB very resonable\n\nProblem : When user with lot of followers creates a post, we need to write to millions of feeds\n            This is handled in next section\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. Handle users who are following large number of users?\n- If a user follows large nos of users, queries to Follow table takes time\n- Now for each of those users, we make large nos of queries to Posts table to build the feed\n- This is called, fan-out on read i.e. a single read req fans out to create many more requests\n- The solution is to pre-compute the feed at the time of write or post creation, rather than at the time of read.\n- This is called fan-out on write - instead of assembling feed when user asks for it, we precompute feed when posts are created.\n- Natural question is what if a user has large nos of followers, well a Product decision is required to limit no of followers, FB does it with limit of 5000 friends\n- As a user with 100k follows doesn't notice if their posts are appearing couple of mins late.\n- Have a PrecomputedFeed table(userId(PK), createdAt(SK), postId[]), if new post created just add to relevant feeds.\n- postId is a list in reverse chronological order, limited to small nos of posts say 200 or so, keeping space minimum.\n- What if user user pageback to 100s of pages and we end on our pre-computed feed? There's always a cap, even google does that, doesn't allows you to reach the end as real users don't do that\n- Even so, we can always query Follow and Posts table as fallback.\n- postId(10 bytes) * 200(posts stored per user) * 2B(total no of user) = 4TB very resonable\n\nProblem : When user with lot of followers creates a post, we need to write to millions of feeds\n            This is handled in next section\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "s0Tv8rTbKWKy7U0WFeHt9",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -3724.3540340848717,
      "y": 2664.751931968924,
      "width": 2187.578369140625,
      "height": 800,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a8",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 81567657,
      "version": 2720,
      "versionNonce": 1355356199,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783527085816,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "2. How to handle users with large number of followers?\n- when post created, need to write to million of feed records\n- allowed eventual consistency(< 1 min)\n\nBad Solution : Blast the requests\n- blast out all the requests at once from post service when a post is created, this means we're trying to write million of feeds with new posts\n- Not workable, due to number of connections that could be made from a single post service host and high latency\n\n\nGood Solution : Async Workers\n- Since we can tolerate some delay b/w post is creation and when its available in feeds, we queue up write requests \n  and have a fleet of workers consume these requests to update feeds.\n- This is async worker threads behind a queue.\n- Amazon's SQS, Kafka works at they ensure at-least once delivery.\n- When post created, add an entry to SQS with postId and creatorUserId\n- Each worker looks up all the followers for that creator and prepend the post to the front of feed entry.\n\nProblems\n    - The throughput of feed workers needs to be enormous, works well for small account with limited followers\n    -  Lot of work for mega accounts with million of followers\n    - Variable amount of work for different users as pushing post to precomputed feed for a user with 1k followers takes less time compared to pushing it to 1M followers, need to breakup these tasks.\n\n\n\nGreat Solution : Async Workers with Hybrid Feeds\n- Add a new field to Follow table \"precomputed\" which is set to false for popular accounts, meaning we won't generate preComputed feed from popular accounts\n- For normal users this is set to true, means our async workers will update their precomputed feed as they are small in number, not costly.\n- Problem was for popularUserId(Justin Bieber) which is present in millions of userId entries in PrecomputedFeed, very costly to prepend their postId list\n- So if precomputed set to false, async workers ignore processing these\n- At read, when users request their feed via Feed Service, we grab their (partially)precomputed feed and merge it with recent posts from those accounts which aren't precomputed, sort them in chronological order and return.\n- This hybrid approach allows us to choose whether we fan out on read or fan out on write on a per-account basis.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "2. How to handle users with large number of followers?\n- when post created, need to write to million of feed records\n- allowed eventual consistency(< 1 min)\n\nBad Solution : Blast the requests\n- blast out all the requests at once from post service when a post is created, this means we're trying to write million of feeds with new posts\n- Not workable, due to number of connections that could be made from a single post service host and high latency\n\n\nGood Solution : Async Workers\n- Since we can tolerate some delay b/w post is creation and when its available in feeds, we queue up write requests \n  and have a fleet of workers consume these requests to update feeds.\n- This is async worker threads behind a queue.\n- Amazon's SQS, Kafka works at they ensure at-least once delivery.\n- When post created, add an entry to SQS with postId and creatorUserId\n- Each worker looks up all the followers for that creator and prepend the post to the front of feed entry.\n\nProblems\n    - The throughput of feed workers needs to be enormous, works well for small account with limited followers\n    -  Lot of work for mega accounts with million of followers\n    - Variable amount of work for different users as pushing post to precomputed feed for a user with 1k followers takes less time compared to pushing it to 1M followers, need to breakup these tasks.\n\n\n\nGreat Solution : Async Workers with Hybrid Feeds\n- Add a new field to Follow table \"precomputed\" which is set to false for popular accounts, meaning we won't generate preComputed feed from popular accounts\n- For normal users this is set to true, means our async workers will update their precomputed feed as they are small in number, not costly.\n- Problem was for popularUserId(Justin Bieber) which is present in millions of userId entries in PrecomputedFeed, very costly to prepend their postId list\n- So if precomputed set to false, async workers ignore processing these\n- At read, when users request their feed via Feed Service, we grab their (partially)precomputed feed and merge it with recent posts from those accounts which aren't precomputed, sort them in chronological order and return.\n- This hybrid approach allows us to choose whether we fan out on read or fan out on write on a per-account basis.\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "7uLfZ3KqlLkhpV3ihpkSc",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -3723.09449039888,
      "y": 3538.1788709071657,
      "width": 1369.59912109375,
      "height": 475,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a9",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1479710087,
      "version": 1317,
      "versionNonce": 86011433,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783532423465,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "3. How to handle uneven reads of Posts?\n- Vast majority of posts will be read for a few days and never read again.\n- For popular posts, the number of reads the post experiences will be massive.\n- Problem is how to solve hot keys issue in our Post Table\n\nGood Solution : Post Cache with Large Keyspace\n- Add a Redis cache between Feed Service and Post table in DynamoDB, so that posts are served directly from cache.\n- Have a long TTL with LRU as posts rarely change.\n- When posts are edited, we invalidate the cache for that postId\n\nProblem : Same hot-key problem, some partitions will have unusually high load.\n\n\nGreat Solution : Redundant Post Cache\n- Instead of a shared cache(where each post lives on exactly one node), we use a replicated cache where every instance can serve any post\n- A LB distributes load, handling the traffic evenly.\n- Meaning a viral post gets spread across all N instances.\n- this does means we may have more cache misses initially i.e. instead of 1 DB query to populate that, we now have N queries\n  But N is much smaller than millions",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "3. How to handle uneven reads of Posts?\n- Vast majority of posts will be read for a few days and never read again.\n- For popular posts, the number of reads the post experiences will be massive.\n- Problem is how to solve hot keys issue in our Post Table\n\nGood Solution : Post Cache with Large Keyspace\n- Add a Redis cache between Feed Service and Post table in DynamoDB, so that posts are served directly from cache.\n- Have a long TTL with LRU as posts rarely change.\n- When posts are edited, we invalidate the cache for that postId\n\nProblem : Same hot-key problem, some partitions will have unusually high load.\n\n\nGreat Solution : Redundant Post Cache\n- Instead of a shared cache(where each post lives on exactly one node), we use a replicated cache where every instance can serve any post\n- A LB distributes load, handling the traffic evenly.\n- Meaning a viral post gets spread across all N instances.\n- this does means we may have more cache misses initially i.e. instead of 1 DB query to populate that, we now have N queries\n  But N is much smaller than millions",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "t7fkjXHSJOfXQws2N5RM3",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -3693.262465033583,
      "y": 1044.431377059139,
      "width": 70.54190293862496,
